  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 255 AM MST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nWe find ourselves in a ridging pattern once again to start the \nwork week with a trough exiting the eastern US. The first in a \nsequence of two longwave ridges builds in throughout the Great \nPlains Monday, accompanied by northwest flow aloft, as we \ninitially sit on the leeward side. This system is then expected \nto weaken quite a bit as the axis passes north of the County \nWarning Area (CWA), resulting in zonal westerly flow. Ensemble \nguidance continues to project stronger ridging to build in \nacross the Rockies that likely ushers in northwest flow as we \nposition east of the axis. Models are beginning to show us under\nnortherly upper-level winds Thursday as the ridge continues its\ntrek across the CONUS. High pressure is forecast to dominate \naloft as well as at the surface through the middle portions of \nnext week. A stationary front is anticipated to linger across \nwestern Kansas and eastern Colorado Tuesday through Thursday. \nDespite the lifting mechanism, precipitation (PoPs) in the form \nof rain are highly unlikely through Thursday\n\nNew developments of this second ridge signal that quiet, \npleasant weather is possible heading into late next week. \nSubsidence plus low moisture content in the atmosphere should \nequate to warmer and drier conditions as we progress through the\nweek. As mentioned above, no rain is currently forecasted \nthrough Thursday. Conditions should be quite nice for this time \nof the year as a warmer trend is projected to continue across \nthe CWA, paired with plentiful sunshine throughout most of the \nfour-day period. Highs in the mid/upper 60s are forecast Monday \nthrough Thursday for the entire CWA but a few spots reaching the\nlow 70s are possible as well. Low temps should be cool and \ncrisp every morning of this long-term period as guidance output \nsuggests mid 30s to mid 40s. There is the possibility of some \nlocations to reach freezing Monday and Tuesday mornings but \nconfidence is low at this time.\n\nAs drier air is likely to take over our atmosphere, Relative \nHumidity (RH) values will be monitored, with respect to fire \nweather weather concerns, through next week. Guidance currently \nhas a general range of 25-45% RH forecasted, but areas west of \nUS-385 in eastern Colorado could see values in the low 20s. \nSurface winds are not likely to be very strong next week either,\nas a range of 5-20 mph sustained winds is probable. Wednesday \nshould be the windiest day across the CWA with gusts up to 30 \nmph possible. Furthermore, GFDI values indicate that dry fuels \nare not an issue due to recent rains from earlier this week. \nTherefore, fire weather should not be much of a concern through \nnext week.",
